+## Revision Model
+
+Every accepted submission becomes an immutable revision of its visit, holding the complete submitted payload, the visit ID, the author's identity, the base revision ID it was started from, and the server receipt time. Revision IDs are assigned by the server and are unique within their visit.
+
+A visit has exactly one **current revision**: the state callers read, and the state a later submission must be based on. The first accepted submission for a visit names no base and becomes both the visit's first revision and its current revision.
+
+Acceptance compares the submission's base revision ID with the visit's current revision:
+
+- **Base is current.** The submission becomes the current revision; the previous current revision remains in history.
+- **Base is not current, but exists for the visit.** The submission is stored as a **competing revision**. The current revision does not move, and the visit carries unresolved review work until a coordinator resolves it. Competing revisions accumulate: every submission based on a revision that is no longer current joins them.
+- **Base does not exist for the visit.** The submission is rejected during validation and nothing is stored.
+
+A visit with no competing revisions is resolved, and its current revision is a usable state for retrieval and export. Competing submissions are recorded in server receipt order; receipt order alone decides which of two submissions from the same base advances the current revision, and it carries no other meaning.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+A submission envelope carries the visit ID, the base revision ID (omitted only for a visit's first submission), the complete visit payload, and the idempotency key. The server records the authenticated author and the receipt time. Field types and the plot reference are validated against the catalog before acceptance, along with the base revision rules above and the presence or absence of an existing visit.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+The acceptance response distinguishes the two outcomes: a submission that became the current revision, and a submission stored as a competing revision. Both are acceptances.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+Retrieval of a visit returns the current revision (ID, payload, author, receipt time), an indication that unresolved review work exists (REC-005), and a summary of each competing revision (ID, base revision, author, receipt time) so a caller can show what is waiting. Submission-history retrieval returns the original submissions in receipt order.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+## Resolution
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A coordinator resolves review work by recording a resolution. A resolution names the competing revisions it resolves — one or more — together with the visit's current revision as the coordinator read it, and results in one new current revision: either one of the named competing revisions, adopted with its payload unchanged, or a coordinator-authored complete payload. Named revisions that are not adopted are declined; they stay in history and never become current.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Every resolution retains the coordinator's identity, the contributing submission IDs, and the outcome (REC-006). A resolution never modifies or removes a stored submission.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+Competing revisions not named by a resolution remain unresolved review work. A resolution that names a current revision no longer current for that visit is rejected, so two coordinators recording outcomes against the same visit cannot both succeed without re-reading it.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+**tablet-sync** sizes local storage and its retry state machine around the envelope. Each local draft retains its base revision ID and is a complete visit payload, so a draft can sit on a tablet through a 48-hour disconnection and still be submittable — acceptance depends only on its base revision. Its submit response must distinguish current from competing, because a worker whose visit went to review has succeeded rather than failed.
+
+**review-export** sizes its coordinator interface and export eligibility around the unresolved-work representation. It provides the comparison view over competing complete visits, offers the resolution operation above, and draws its export set only from visits with no unresolved review work. A resolved visit exports its current revision, and that visit's resolution record carries the exported value back to the submissions it came from.
+
+Both link back to this node rather than inventing concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy for concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: each submission names its base revision, and competing revisions go to coordinator review | Field-aware three-way merge (base snapshot plus structured changes, with disjoint field edits combined automatically); arrival-order overwrite, which is the spreadsheet behaviour being replaced | [concurrent-visit-edits](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md) — a larger review queue is preferred over maintained merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing. |
+| What a resolution may produce | One named competing revision adopted verbatim, or a coordinator-authored complete payload | Adopt-one-only, which would force a coordinator to discard one side of an unrelated-field conflict; field-level combination performed by the server | Two coordinators must be able to keep both sides of a conflict without a merge engine. Composition stays human and explicit, and every outcome names its contributing submissions. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrent visit edits are reconciled as whole-visit revisions, with competing revisions resolved by a coordinator — see [concurrent-visit-edits](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md).
+
+### Deferred
+1. Retention and archival of superseded, declined, and competing revisions across seasons. Storage grows with every season's ~6,000 visits plus their competing revisions until a retention policy exists.
+2. Enforcement of who may resolve. The design assumes an authenticated coordinator; mapping the team's accounts to that role follows whatever identity approach the deployment already uses.
